    pr_ref:  # 5df9
    description: "Left open by the entry-point tie order and cap change of PR #2203 (ties at equal rank_score order by detection confidence, then language weight, then effective out-degree, then path / line / name / id; a cap cut inside a tied group deals its slots round-robin across source files). The owner ruled that rank_score, its ceiling and the schema stay as they are; these follow from that.\n\n1. A PROGRAM'S main NOW SORTS AFTER ROUTES AMONG TIES. A convention `main` (Go `func main`, a Python `__main__` guard) carries the confidence literal 0.80; a framework route carries 0.90 or 0.95, a manifest-declared script 0.99. With confidence as the first tie-break, among entries saturated at rank_score 1.0 every route precedes every main. Measured before / after on 23 repositories, a main that was in the top 3 of the list and no longer is: alertmanager positions 0, 1 -> 13, 14; kserve 0, 1, 2 -> 17, 18, 19; mini-redis 1 -> 3; mitmproxy 0, 1, 2 -> 36, 37, 38; podman 0, 1, 2 -> 82, 83, 84; prometheus 0, 1 -> 48, 49. Visible in the JSON entry list, `slice --list-entries` and the sketch Structure tree's entry files. Not affected: the sketch's CLI & Scripts group; `slice --entry auto` (same pick before and after on alertmanager, prometheus, kserve). The confidence literals were set as detection confidence, not as a ranking key. Question: is 0.80 right for a Go `func main` in `package main`, or should the tie order put a program's main first by another key?\n\n2. ROUTES JUST UNDER 1.0 ARE OUTSIDE THE TIED GROUP. On thc the Express `GET /health` scores 0.9896 and is still dropped by the cap; `POST /scan` (1.0) is now kept. The round-robin deals only entries of exactly the cut's score.\n\n3. THE SKETCH'S PER-GROUP BUDGET CUT IS A SECOND TRUNCATION AND IS NOT ROUND-ROBIN: it keeps the first entries of the group in list order.\n\n4. THE ROUTE-LABEL DEDUP sorts same-label duplicates by rank_score only, so among duplicates of equal score the one kept still depends on detection order.\n\n5. `_connectivity_fallback` (top 5 by out-degree) and compact.py's force-include order (-confidence, symbol_id) keep orders of their own.\n\nMeasurement record: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/stable_id_items_review_11152026/pr3/mapaj/TABLE.txt."  # 5df9
    fields: {}  # 5df9
